WEBVTT

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.240
The Jack Benny program with his special guest stars,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.090
Mr. and Mrs. Ronald Coleman.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.400
Presented by Lucky Strike.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.090
and he takes a bath in this stuff every day

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.530
and he still don't look any younger.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.960
I guess I better get his breakfast ready.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.180
morning I didn't know you're up yet I was just getting ready to bring your

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.220
breakfast up your room never mind I'll eat right down here okay how'd you sleep

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.460
last night how'd I sleep I wish I knew I'm gonna stop getting undressed in the

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.950
dark why I don't know that I tossed and turned all night I put my pajamas on

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:30.000
backwards no no they always creep up on me and when I wake up in the morning I look like I'm

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.050
wearing an old life preserver.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.870
What do you got all those books out for?

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.310
Oh, I had to take them off the shelf so I could clean them.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.530
Oh, good, good.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.610
Rochester, how long did you boil this egg?

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.750
A minute and a half.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.490
A minute and a half, is that all?

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.490
Yeah, when you told me you got them from a fire cell,

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:56.840
I figured that's all they needed.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.430
What's this?

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.000
Smoked ham, same cell.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:07.970
what are you laughing at this bread was ridiculous I had to scrape it before I

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:15.190
put it in the toaster well they were all such bargains I just couldn't turn them

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.570
down say boss if you tell me what time you'll be home from work this afternoon

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.430
I'll have your milk bath already for you no no after yesterday I'm not gonna take

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:30.000
those baths anymore well I thought you enjoyed bathing in milk what happened I splashed the

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.380
around so much the milk turned to butter and I could hardly get out of the tub.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:39.320
Rochester, from now on, let's go back to the plain, ordinary, lavender bubble bath.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.320
Yes.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.170
Oh, my goodness, look what time it is.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.590
Rochester, I won't be able to finish my breakfast.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.330
I'll grab a bite to eat at the studio.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.790
Look, and if my director calls, tell him I'm on my way, will you?

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:53.920
Yes, sir.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.280
I better get these books put back.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.190
Let's see, this stack goes up here.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.220
You can't take it with you by Kauffman and Hart.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:13.950
You can't take it with you by Kauffman and Hart.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:19.800
You can't take it with you by Kauffman and Hart.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:27.070
I'll find a way by Jack Benny.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.000
He probably will by Kauffman and Hart.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.240
on the Mr. Benny's Diaries.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.010
1945.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:45.920
1945.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.380
Yeah, one little peek won't hurt anybody.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.860
This page looks like it might be interesting.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.440
August 15th, 1945.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:01.900
Dear Diary,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.770
Tonight, my next-door neighbors, Mr. and Mrs. Ronald Coleman, invited me to have dinner at their home.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:14.830
Ronnie and Benita were so thrilled that I accepted their invitation.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.710
Mr. and Mrs. Ronald Coleman invited Mr. Benning to dinner?

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.760
That's funny, I don't...

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.000
Oh yeah, now I remember!

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.510
That's the day that Mr. and Mrs. Don Wilson and Mrs. Livingston were over here playing bridge.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:42.730
Betty, don't forget you got a dinner appointment at 8 o'clock.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.110
Yes, yes, I know.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:45.980
I'll see.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:47.570
A dinner appointment?

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:48.430
Where are you going?

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.080
Oh, Mr. and Mrs. Ronald Coleman invited me to dinner tonight.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.070
They live next door, you know.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.930
You invited to the Coleman's?

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.650
- Well, what's so strange about that?

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.220
We've been neighbors for a long, long time.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.560
Of course, for a while there,

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.120
my success in pictures came between us, you know.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.560
I don't wanna be late because they went through

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.760
all that bother sending me an invitation and everything.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.920
I don't wanna keep 'em waiting.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.960
- Jack, you mean they sent you an invitation

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.290
and they live right next door?

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.940
- Well, that would be the dignified thing to do.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.480
- Jack, could I see that invitation

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.250
you got from the Coleman's?

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.000
- Well, of course you could.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:38.160
and see it. I wish Rochester wouldn't be so careless with my mail till I found that out

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.830
on the back porch. Dear Jack, glad to know you are back in America. Benita and I would

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:50.170
love to have you for cocktails and dinner Sunday evening. We'll expect you around eight.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:06:00.000
Regards, Ronnie. You satisfied? Jack, what does he mean, back in America? Well, for my

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.510
trip. My trip last summer to Europe. I still don't understand it. What's there to

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:13.270
understand? Look, I've been invited to some of the best homes in Beverly Hills.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:22.090
Well, I have. No, Jack. Well, I just read in one of the columns that last week you were a guest

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:30.000
at Gary Cooper's birthday party. Some guest. He was waiting on tables. I just did that for a

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.250
Gag, eh?

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.710
Why do the people put tips under their plates?

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.010
A gag, too.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.630
Then why'd you pick up the tips?

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.500
Because I can go along with a gag, sister.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.220
Sure hurt.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.620
I got screams every time I picked up a tip, they yelled.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.240
Boss, you better hurry up.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:53.880
It's getting late.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.640
The Coleman's always dressed for dinner,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.780
so lay out my white tie and tails.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.000
Yes, sir.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:06.500
Now, let's finish the game because I want to...

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.980
Of course, you better get doing this thing.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.900
I think I'll be five minutes late.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.240
No one will make an impression on the Colemans.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.950
They're almost as nervous as I am.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:49.000
- Yes, Benita, what is it?

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.520
- Think it's time you put those stamps away

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.890
and got dressed for dinner?

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.820
You know, we have a guest for dinner tonight.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.080
- A guest tonight who?

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.930
- Well, Jack, of course.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.000
Jack Wellington from London.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:06.490
I forgot good old Wellington well I'm glad you remembered to mail him the

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:10.500
letter I wrote well I didn't as a matter of fact I couldn't find it anywhere I

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.260
think it must have blown out of the window or something

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:19.390
anyhow I phoned him instead splendid splendid so don't you think you ought to

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:24.200
get dressed dressed oh my dear not for Wellington these clothes are good enough

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:30.000
for him besides he hates anything formal change either then but I do think it

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:34.720
occasion calls for some champagne why don't you get some out darling then we can use that lovely

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.540
silver bucket Wellington gave us when we first got married I'll go and get it he'll be thrilled

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:53.960
to see it again yes so will I what the comedian who lives next door borrowed it now now Benita

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:59.830
there's no use getting upset it seems he was giving a party for his sponsor and wanted to impress him

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.440
Jack Benny serving champagne I don't believe it

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:10.430
you will if he's thoughtful enough to return our empty bottle

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:15.920
also and three trays of ice cube

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.410
now how could you encourage him like that

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.000
encouraging my dear girl I wasn't even here

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.990
he came in through the window helped himself and left a note

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.000
use the other ice bucket

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:36.030
hope things will be quiet over there while Wellington's here why have they

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:40.410
been noisy again oh my dear this morning it was terrible mr. Benny was shouting

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:45.210
at that ballot of his what's his name again and you know it's the same as the

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:50.650
city in England Liverpool

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:59.670
Southampton Manchester

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:05.290
Manchester well mr. Benny was shouting at Manchester about his coffee was too weak

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:09.130
and Manchester was shouting back to say that he'd been telling him for years

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:16.640
you're not supposed to use the coffee grounds more than one yes I've heard some

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.540
of those sessions but you know they're mild when you compare them with that

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:26.290
Frank Rembley fellow I remember years ago when he rang our front doorbell on

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:30.000
the stroke of midnight to wish me a happy new year well I think that was

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.150
- On August the 5th?

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.190
- Well, that's just a little odd.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:45.180
- Tell me, darling, does this Remley person

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.180
have something to do with the Benny program?

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.800
- Yes, I understand he's in the orchestra.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.350
- Oh, he's a musician.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.550
- No, no, he's just in the orchestra.

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:56.590
- I beg your pardon, Mr. Coleman.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.010
- Oh, yes, Sherwood.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.000
- Mr. Jack Wellington has arrived.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:02.340
- Oh, good, good. - Fine, just check on the dinner,

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:07.150
will you see, shall we?

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:10.150
- There he is. - Wellington, old boy.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:13.150
How are you? - Oh, I'm so glad to see you, Jack.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.290
- Glad to see you too, dear. - Let me take your coat.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:16.400
- Oh, thank you.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:17.780
You know, I came straight from the clubs.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:19.740
I hope you don't mind my being dressed like this.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.620
- Oh, of course not. - Much cozier as we are.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:24.930
By the way, Ronnie, I was always on the impression

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.830
that the part of Beverly Hills that you two live in

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:30.000
was exclusively a residential area, and yet right now,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.960
next door to you, I notice there's quite a

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:33.660
busy used car dealer.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:35.780
Oh, no, there's Jack Benny's house.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.380
He tore up the front lawn and put in a parking lot.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:41.740
You mean the fellow on the wireless?

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.910
Yes. I just the other night

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.150
we tuned in on his program quite by accident

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:48.130
and it was the most...

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.150
Now, now, let's not talk about that

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:50.710
before dinner.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.350
Come on, Wellington, come into the library and have a drink.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.790
Good. I want to hear all your news.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:58.500
How you found things on the continent,

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.000
all about your Indian trip,

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:02.510
Tell me now, how are things in the elephant set?

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:06.010
I had my usual bad luck in Puna, but that's rather a long story.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.110
Well, we had the whole evening together.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.710
So let's have a cocktail first, shall we?

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.110
I should say so.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:17.880
Benita, this is one of the glasses I sent you from Rangoon.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.770
They're in my most treasured possession.

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.730
You know, no one will believe us when I tell them

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.140
that there are only 11 of those glasses in the entire world.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:30.000
Well, it's absolutely true. The King of Siam has five.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:33.210
- And you have six.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.050
- Let me see if I can remember that recipe

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:36.520
you brought back from a lair.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.760
- There's a gentleman at the door, sir.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:39.990
Here's his card.

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:42.040
- Card?

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.300
Jack Benny.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.010
- Jack Benny?

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:47.660
Here.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.870
Oh, maybe he's come to return the ice bucket.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.770
- Well, Sherwood.

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:56.850
Tell Mr. Benny, oh, tell him I'll call him tomorrow.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:58.520
- Explain that we have a guest for dinner

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.000
and that'll take care of him.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.000
He said you were expecting him for dinner.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:06.890
Dinner?

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:09.870
Benita, did you invite Jack Benny for dinner?

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:10.870
Well, no.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:11.920
Are you sure you didn't?

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:13.920
I'm positive.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.950
Show him in and set another place for dinner.

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:24.950
Yes, sir.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.960
Now, let's get back to your recipe, Wellington.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.960
I think after a few drops...

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.280
Mr. and Mrs. Coleman in the library, may I have your hat, sir?

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.440
Oh, yes, yes.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.250
Oh, just a moment.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.590
Ronnie!

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:55.790
Good to see you.

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.760
Well, they say it's good to see you, Jack.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:00.000
Benita!

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.160
- To see you, to see you, I mean, it's nice, it's nice.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.100
- You know what?

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.960
- Well, I, I mean, I wanted to meet an old friend of ours.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.000
Mr. Jack Wellington, this is Mr. Benny.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.610
- I'm glad to meet you, Mr. Benny.

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:12.950
- Well, I didn't expect anyone else here for dinner,

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:15.150
but then that's just like the Coleman's,

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:16.990
always room for one more.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:27.010
- Yes, sir.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:33.940
give you a cocktail no no thank you I'll just sit here and read a magazine while

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.960
you three go and get dressed

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:46.100
no my dear fellow this is quite informal informal Ronnie I can't tell you how

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:56.020
happy I am to hear you say that my shoes are killing

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.420
Ronnie, I think I could use a cocktail.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:12.660
Mr. Wellington, you're from England, aren't you?

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.010
Why, yes. As a matter of fact, I am.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.910
And you were just about to tell us, how are things over there?

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:20.190
Oh, it's very exciting. The Tory season is in full swing,

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:22.250
and Lance Olivier has just made a smashing success

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:24.200
with his new film, Henry V.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.200
Good. Well, I only hope I can get over there next year.

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:28.900
I like to be there in the spring.

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.000
Oh, sir.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.740
- So do I, everything looks so lovely in the spring.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.740
Do you remember the river at Pangborn?

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:36.640
Thank you, darling.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.140
- Ah, the Thames.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.140
Yes, the jolly old Thames.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:41.680
- Yes.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.680
- You know, I always take them when my stomach is upset.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.160
- Thames, Thames.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:00.000
Have you read any good books lately?

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.870
Why, yes, dear, you're suggesting I reread The Decline and Fall,

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:05.750
and believe it or not, I found it even more absorbing the second time.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:07.670
Well, I think that's off in the way.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:13.750
I've just reread Burton's Anatomy of Melancholy, and, you know, it's very amusing.

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:15.580
Well, it's not funny enough for me.

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:20.330
I like something more full-blooded, now you take the old Greek tragedies.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:27.030
The torments of Oedipus, the vengeance of Electra, the agonies of Medea.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:42.630
Have any of you noticed the frightful mess that little orphan Annie has been in lately?

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:54.420
Yes, sir.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:02.790
"Yep."

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:08.220
"I think we can't do it."

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.800
"Yes, sir."

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:20.930
"You said it."

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.320
"Dinner is served."

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:26.360
"Thank you, Sherwood."

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:27.970
"Shall we go into the dining room?"

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:28.770
"Come on, Jack."

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:30.000
"Oh, I'm hungry."

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.030
I need you to have dinner with us today.

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:35.480
I beg your pardon, Mr. Benny.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:52.190
He certainly was.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.080
You want some more coffee?

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:55.080
No, thank you, dear.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.080
You know, that dinner was absolutely wonderful.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.090
Well, I'm very glad you enjoyed it.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:17:59.090
I have an idea.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:00.000
I shall go and see.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.150
find that bottle of old brandy. Oh good. I'll come and get the glasses. Help yourself to

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:14.980
coffee, Mr. Bell. I believe I will have some. Let me do it for you. Thank you. You know,

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:21.670
Wellington, I thought it was awfully decent of you to say that you enjoyed the dinner tonight.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:30.000
But I did. Well, it was all right, but it seems to me that they could have had bigger chicken.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:45.320
with squab. Benita, where in the world is that bottle of brandy? Oh, let me see. It's

00:18:45.320 --> 00:19:00.000
on the lower shelf on the left. I remember it was right next to your ice bucket. Benita,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.000
I'm telling Wellington here how much I enjoyed your squab.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:06.000
Good.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.560
Here we are.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:12.560
Wellington, before you leave town, you ought to come down to the club and I'll play your game of tennis.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:16.560
I'm afraid there's a tournament going on there right now, but we could go over to Bel Air.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:17.560
Here you are.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:18.450
Thank you, dear.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:19.400
Mr. Benny.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.500
Thank you.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:23.390
Now, Ronnie, come on, let's have a toast.

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:24.390
Yes, yes, a toast.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:26.390
How did that speech go in Henry V?

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:29.280
Well, you mean once more onto the breach?

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.000
That's it.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:34.860
human shows here the metal of your posture. I see you stand like greyhounds in

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:40.810
the slips training upon the start. The game's afoot, follow your spirit and upon

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:52.050
this charge cry, God for Harry, England and St. George!

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:19.930
That's what they did in the picture.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:30.000
Well, that makes us even with the King of Siam.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:54.280
Charades and liven things up.

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.290
I don't think so, Jack. It's getting a little late.

00:20:57.290 --> 00:21:00.000
Oh, my goodness, I had no idea how late it was.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:29.540
getting all the late late it's it's only 9 30. yes sir yep time marches on yes sir yup

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:35.290
Well, I guess I'd better be running along.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:36.350
Oh, what a pity.

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:37.550
I'll get your coats.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.410
Well, goodbye, Mr. Wellington.

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:52.490
It was a pleasure.

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:56.540
Goodbye, Mr. Benny, and with my luck, we'll probably meet again.

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.000
Yes, yes, thank you.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:02.030
Benita, I had such a wonderful time.

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:03.710
Well, we were happy you came.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:04.770
Thank you. Goodbye.

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:19.160
Your hat.

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.150
You?

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:23.150
Oh, my gloves.

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:25.540
They're in your hats.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:28.360
Oh, yes, yes.

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:42.460
Ronnie, I had a wonderful evening, and I'm terribly sorry about Wellington.

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:44.870
Wellington? What do you mean?

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:49.020
Well, I know how loyal you are to your friends,

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:53.260
but I think it's awful the way one man can put a damper on a whole party.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:58.090
You know, Jack, there's a lot of truth in what you say.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:00.000
Okay.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:02.310
Goodbye, Benita.

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:03.550
Good night.

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:05.110
Good night, Ronnie.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:05.930
Good night, Jack.

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:10.000
I'm not out yet.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:17.410
Oh, on me.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.410
Well, good-bye, Ronnie.

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:20.410
Hey, good-bye, Jack.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:21.410
Good-bye.

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:22.410
Good night.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.410
Can it be the breeze that fills me?

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.990
Good night, Diary.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:59.670
...this afternoon.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:01.900
Didn't you go to the Coleman's?

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:03.340
Well, of course I did.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:06.080
Oh, what are you doing home so early?

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:09.550
Well, I thought it was the nice thing to do.

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:11.800
You know, they were so tired.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:14.030
Tired at 9:30?

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:15.560
9:30.

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:18.160
They started yawning 10 minutes after I got there.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:22.620
Two.

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:24.120
Of course I am.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:25.760
But I'm not going right to sleep.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.300
I'm going to read a while.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:28.730
What are you going to read?

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:30.000
Oh, I don't know.

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:37.050
might read Decline and Fall or Burton's Anatomy of Melancholy. I understand it's

00:24:37.050 --> 00:24:49.670
quite amusing. Jack, did you have a sip of brandy? Brandy? Yes. I thought so.

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:54.580
Good night.

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.950
Jack Benny's next television show will be in two weeks.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:38.930
Remember one week from tonight on this same station,

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:41.030
be sure and watch Anne Southern in Private Secretary.
